            <ArticleTitle>Associated trends in sedentary behavior and BMI among Chinese school children and adolescents in seven diverse Chinese provinces.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>342-50</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1007/s12529-011-9177-2</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Overweight and obesity is becoming a public health concern in China.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">We examined associated trends in sedentary behavior and body mass index (BMI) among school children and adolescents from seven diverse Chinese provinces from 1997 to 2004.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHOD" NlmCategory="METHODS">Sequential multiple regressions were conducted with combined cross-sectional data derived from the China Health and Nutrition Survey (N = 5,497) to assess relationships between sedentary behavior and BMI.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Between 1997 and 2004, the amount of time children (6- to 11-year-olds) spent engaging in sedentary behavior increased from 1.71 to 3.14 h/day (p &lt; 0.05), while the amount of time adolescents (12- to 18-year-olds) spent engaging in sedentary behavior increased from 1.71 to 3.50 h/day (p &lt; 0.05). Studying before and after school and watching TV/videos each accounted for about half the increase in sedentary behavior. Both watching TV/videos and studying before and after school were significantly and positively associated with BMI in males, but not in females.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">With the growing amount of time Chinese school children and adolescents study outside of school and watch TV/videos, Chinese youth are increasingly likely to become more overweight unless population-based prevention actions are taken.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
